{"name":"Project updates delayed","status":"resolved","created_at":"2026-10-22T15:28:03.986Z","updated_at":"2026-11-16T16:28:44.234Z","monitoring_at":"2026-10-23T00:55:18.035Z","resolved_at":"2026-10-23T02:30:25.221Z","impact":"minor","shortlink":"https://stspg.io/sx51v1tlpqjc","scheduled_for":null,"scheduled_until":null,"scheduled_remind_prior":false,"scheduled_reminded_at":null,"impact_override":"minor","scheduled_auto_in_progress":false,"scheduled_auto_completed":false,"metadata":{},"started_at":"2026-10-22T15:28:03.979Z","reminder_intervals":null,"id":"wq8305lppdc2","page_id":"kctbh9vrtdwd","incident_updates":[{"status":"resolved","body":"On October 22, 2026, between 14:55 and 17:28 UTC, search on GitHub.com was degraded. Search-backed features in Issues, Pull Requests, Projects, Actions, Packages, and some REST API endpoints returned errors or incomplete results. On average, 21% of search requests failed, peaking at 52%. Running Actions workflows, uploading and downloading packages, and Git operations were not affected. \u003cbr /\u003e\u003cbr /\u003eThis was due to the proxies that route requests to our search clusters running out of CPU while setting up encrypted connections. Requests that timed out were retried, which added more load and kept the proxies overloaded. The search clusters themselves remained healthy. \u003cbr /\u003e\u003cbr /\u003eWe mitigated the incident by adding proxy capacity, after which search recovered at 17:28 UTC. \u003cbr /\u003e\u003cbr /\u003eWe have started reducing the CPU cost of these encrypted connections. We are also working to make search retries adjustable during an incident without a deployment, and to alert on search failures sooner. Together these will reduce our time to detection and mitigation of issues like this one in the future.","created_at":"2026-10-23T02:30:25.221Z","wants_twitter_update":false,"twitter_updated_at":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-16T16:28:44.228Z","display_at":"2026-10-23T02:30:25.221Z","deliver_not